        ":question/id": "9e69ff1d-1e24-5581-b30c-71969f46296b",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "If $f(x)=\\frac{2 x}{\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}$, find $f^{\\prime}(x)$ wherever the original quotient is defined.\n\n$f^{\\prime}(x)=$ {{field-1}}.",
        ":question/worked-solution": "In this case, the expression for $f(x)$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=2 x$ and $v(x)=\\sin{\\left(x \\right)}.$\n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f^{\\prime}(x)&=\\frac{u^{\\prime}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot v^{\\prime}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2 x)^{\\prime}\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{\\prime}}{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{(2)\\cdot (\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})-(2 x)\\cdot (\\cos{\\left(x \\right)})}{(\\sin{\\left(x \\right)})^{2}}\\\\ &=\\frac{- 2 x \\cos{\\left(x \\right)} + 2 \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}}{\\sin^2 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
          {
            ":answer-field/id": "f70208de-0e25-52b3-9f65-37d3fdd1dbb9",
